# TRANSCRIPT-GUARDS: the `.py.txt` rename closed the import, not the launcher

The record-guard rule was written against `*.py`. On the day it landed it reported this tree clean
while **nineteen as-run transcripts across five records reached the model and one carried a
guard**. Sixteen of the nineteen load the checkpoint.

`python probe.as-run.py.txt` runs the file exactly as a `.py` would. The rename was made so a
record copy could not be *imported*, and it did fix that; it never touched running, and nothing
said so.

## What changed

`_record_sources` finds `*.py` and `*.as-run.py.txt` alike, so `_guarded_record_scripts` covers
both. Everything downstream is unchanged: the same AST sites decide whether the rule applies, the
same structural guard check, the same `compile()`, the same execution under stub packages.

Eighteen transcripts gained the standard refusal. The nineteenth,
`TRAIN-COST-2026-09-05/train_cost_probe.as-run.py.txt`, already had one — it was copied from a
script that carried it.

| record | guarded | of which load the checkpoint |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| WP12-BROADCAST-HEADS-2026-09-06 | 12 | 12 |
| RETRIEVAL-CHANNELS-2026-09-06 | 3 | 3 |
| ATTENTION-TERM-2026-09-08 | 2 | 0 |
| RECURRENCE-EXPONENT-2026-09-07 | 1 | 1 |

Two of them are mine, from this evening. I wrote unguarded, runnable, model-reaching transcripts
into a record hours after landing the rule that exists to stop exactly that, and the rule did not
catch them because of the suffix.

## Byte-exactness, which is the real cost

No manifest pins a transcript's bytes, so there was nothing to update in that sense. The stronger
claim is in the records' own prose: these files are described as **byte-exact** copies of what ran.
They are not any more.

Each affected record now carries a dated declaration with the pre- and post-guard sha256 of every
file it holds, so the original bytes remain verifiable, and `TRAIN-COST-2026-09-05` carries a note
saying its own item (4) still holds locally but no longer tree-wide. Full digests are in
`research/records/GUARD-HASHES-2026-09-08.json`, and every one was checked against the file on
disk after the guards went in.

## A slip of mine during this work, disclosed

While mutation-testing I removed a guard from `singles.as-run.py.txt`, restored the file with
`git checkout` — which reverts to HEAD, i.e. to the *unguarded* version rather than to my working
state — and then ran it directly to check the refusal. **It was unguarded and it loads the
checkpoint.** It died at line 10 on `sys.argv[1]` before any MLX import, so nothing loaded and no
lock was taken; I checked the process table and the lock path immediately afterwards and both were
clear. That was luck rather than design.

The rule's own execution check runs these files under stub `mlx`, `mlx_lm` and `local_llm_lab`
packages precisely so a failed guard cannot reach the model, and running one by hand bypassed
that. The mutation was redone with a file copy instead of `git checkout`, and the guard's digest
re-verified against the declaration afterwards.

